family="monospace" font-size="8.5" fill="#9CA8B8" font-weight="normal" letter-spacing="0.4">as it stands today</text><text x="544" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace" font-size="10.5" fill="#E8F0FA" font-weight="bold" letter-spacing="1.2">AS-BUILT DRAWING</text><text x="544" y="276" text-anchor="middle" font-family="monospace" font-size="8.5" fill="#9CA8B8" font-weight="normal" letter-spacing="0.4">measured and drawn to scale</text></svg></div><figcaption style="text-align:center;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.85rem;margin-top:0.6rem;">An as-built is your real building, measured and put on paper.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>A simple way to think about it</h2>

  <p>Think of a map. A map is only useful if it matches the roads that are really there. If a road was moved ten years ago and the map was never updated, the map will send you the wrong way.</p>

  <p>An as-built drawing is an up-to-date map of your building. It shows where every wall, door, and window really is, and how big every room really is.</p>

  <h2>What is in a set of as-built drawings</h2>

  <p>A set can be one drawing or several. You only order the ones your project needs. These are the most common:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Floor plan.</strong> The building seen from above, as if the roof were lifted off. It shows walls, doors, windows, and room sizes. This is the one almost every project needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Site plan.</strong> The whole property seen from above: the building, the lot lines, the driveway, and how far the building sits from each edge of the lot.</li>
    <li><strong>Elevations.</strong> Flat views of the outside faces of the building, or of inside walls. They show heights, windows, doors, and rooflines.</li>
    <li><strong>Roof plan.</strong> The roof seen from above, with its slopes and edges.</li>
    <li><strong>Ceiling plan.</strong> Where the lights, vents, and ceiling heights are.</li>
    <li><strong>Sections.</strong> The building sliced open like a cake, so you can see floor and ceiling heights.</li>
  </ul>

