  <h2>Who needs as-built drawings</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Homeowners</strong> planning a remodel, an addition, or an ADU.</li>
    <li><strong>Architects and designers</strong> who need an accurate starting point to design from.</li>
    <li><strong>Contractors</strong> who need real sizes to price and build the work.</li>
    <li><strong>Property owners and managers</strong> who lease space or keep records of their buildings.</li>
    <li><strong>Business owners</strong> moving into a new space and laying it out.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If you are about to change, permit, lease, or sell a building, somebody on the project is going to need to know what is there. That is what the as-built answers.</p>

  <h2>How as-builts are made</h2>

  <p>It happens in two steps.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: we measure the building.</strong> We walk through with a 3D laser scanner. It records millions of measurements as we walk. For a typical home this takes about 30 minutes. Nothing is damaged and nothing has to be moved out.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: we draw it.</strong> Back at the office, we turn those measurements into clean drawings. You get them as PDF files you can print and CAD files your architect or contractor can work in.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Why not just use a tape measure?</div>
    <p>A tape measure captures one number at a time, and it is easy to miss one or write one down wrong. A scanner captures the whole room at once, so nothing is forgotten and nobody has to come back for a missing measurement.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>

  <h3>Are as-builts the same as blueprints?</h3>

  <p>No. Blueprints show what was planned before the building was built. As-builts show what is really there now. The two are often different. We explain why in <a href="/resources/as-built-drawings-vs-original-plans/">As-Built Drawings vs. Original Plans</a>.</p>

  <h3>I already have old plans. Do I still need as-builts?</h3>

  <p>Old plans are helpful, but most buildings have changed since they were drawn. If you are spending real money on a project, it is worth checking the plans against the building first.</p>

  <h3>How much do they cost?</h3>

  <p>A floor plan starts at $750. The price depends on the size of the building and which drawings you need. You can get a price in about a minute with our <a href="/quote/">instant quote calculator</a>.</p>

  <h3>How long does it take?</h3>

  <p>Floor plans and site plans are delivered within 3 days of the site visit. Elevations take about 5 business days longer.</p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>

  <p>As-built drawings are an accurate, current picture of your building. They are the first thing a project needs, because every decision after that depends on them. Read more in <a href="/resources/why-every-project-starts-with-as-builts/">Why Every Building Project Starts With As-Built Drawings</a>.</p>
